"""v18 starts from the author's immutable v5, then adds completed hypothesis checks."""
from pathlib import Path
import copy,hashlib,importlib.util,json,re,xml.etree.ElementTree as E
import pandas as pd
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;COMA=ROOT.parents[1]
spec=importlib.util.spec_from_file_location('previous',ROOT.parent/'revision_v17/build_revision.py');previous=importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec);spec.loader.exec_module(previous)
v,h,NS,W=previous.v,previous.h,previous.NS,previous.W
SOURCE=COMA/'2026_07_09_Paper_2_edited-v5_NF_3.docx'
MAIN=COMA/'2026_10_13_Paper_2_edited-v18_v5_based_hypothesis_checks.docx'
SI=ROOT/'Supplementary_Information_v18.docx';AN=ROOT/'analysis'
TITLE='Expert Judgement as Supervision: Human and Quantitative Guidance for Antibacterial Molecular Representations'
def sha(path):return hashlib.sha256(Path(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()
def main():
 files,tree,body=v.read_doc(SOURCE);old=list(body);normal=old[14]
 ff,ft,fb=v.read_doc(previous.MAIN)
 template=next(t for t in fb.findall('w:tbl',NS) if len(t.find('w:tr',NS).findall('w:tc',NS))==5)
 # Import styles used by the new five-column tables; the original tables stay native.
 styles=E.fromstring(files['word/styles.xml']);ids={x.get('{'+W+'}styleId') for x in styles}
 for node in E.fromstring(ff['word/styles.xml']):
  sid=node.get('{'+W+'}styleId')
  if sid and sid not in ids:styles.append(copy.deepcopy(node));ids.add(sid)
 files['word/styles.xml']=h.serialize(styles);v.add_styles(files)
 def p(text,level=None):return v.paragraph(normal,text,level,marked=text.startswith('[COAUTHORS'))
 def metric(arm,encoding,schema):
  a=summary[(summary.arm==arm)&(summary.encoding==encoding)&(summary.schema==schema)&(summary.task=='pooled')];assert len(a)==1;return a.iloc[0]
 def ci(comparison,schema,unit='scaffold'):
  row=contrasts[(contrasts.comparison==comparison)&(contrasts.schema==schema)&(contrasts.unit==unit)].iloc[0]
  return f'{row.delta_RMSE:+.3f} [{row.ci_low:+.3f}, {row.ci_high:+.3f}]'
 def row(arm,encoding,label,seeds):return [label,seeds]+[f'{metric(arm,encoding,s).RMSE:.3f}' for s in ['cation','scaffold','full-QAC105']]
 summary=pd.read_csv(AN/'summary.csv');contrasts=pd.read_csv(AN/'contrasts.csv');modern=pd.read_csv(AN/'modern_metrics.csv')
 humanfields=[copy.deepcopy(x) for x in fb if h.text(x).startswith('[COAUTHORS H')]
 datafields=[copy.deepcopy(x) for x in fb if h.text(x).startswith('[COAUTHORS D')]
 assert len(humanfields)==7 and len(datafields)==2
 replacements={
 0:TITLE,
 8:'Human judgements may offer a compact supervisory signal for quantitative molecular prediction, but their contribution must be separated from model adaptation and evaluation design. We study a frozen MolFormer representation followed by a 768→512→384→256 encoder guided by expert Good/Bad calls and a task-aware CatBoost decoder for four antibacterial endpoints. The original v5 analysis reports human-guided R² values of 0.75, 0.68, 0.59 and 0.77, comparable to the corresponding reported quantitative-guidance results of 0.78, 0.68, 0.58 and 0.77. We retain those findings and their notebook provenance, then test the associated hypotheses on 105 QACs and 17 compounds from four new scaffold families. Paired compact-encoder controls, task-code substitutions, modern tabular models and separate backbone-adaptation experiments reveal dependence on the representation, split and prediction route. Human-guided features improve OOD loss relative to raw features in several configurations, while compact-encoder shuffled-label controls do not establish a human-specific benefit. These results motivate inexpensive human guidance as a candidate source of representation supervision and define the additional evidence needed to attribute its predictive value to annotation content.',
 10:'Quaternary ammonium compounds; antibacterial activity; human supervision; molecular representation; contrastive learning; task encoding; out-of-distribution prediction',
 13:'Antimicrobial resistance is a crucial challenge for modern society, compounded by the difficulty of discovering and developing new antibacterial agents (1,2). Historical WHO analyses documented a limited innovative pipeline (3,4), and the 2024 Bacterial Priority Pathogens List identifies organisms requiring sustained research attention (5). In the WHO analysis with a February 2025 cutoff, 90 antibacterial products were in clinical development; among the 27 antibiotics targeting bacterial priority pathogens, 11 met at least one innovation criterion (6,7). These figures describe the systemic antibacterial pipeline rather than the topical biocides studied here. They nevertheless underline the broader need for reliable experimental and computational strategies for antibacterial discovery.',
 15:'A compelling strategy for molecular prediction is to incorporate domain knowledge and expert guidance into the modelling workflow. Physics-informed neural networks illustrate how physical constraints can restrict a model’s admissible solutions (22,23). Related molecular approaches use chemical substructures, physicochemical priors or biological relationships to shape architectures and objectives (24–26). These mechanisms differ from human annotation, yet they motivate the same practical question: which form of prior information helps a model learn from a limited chemical series? For antibacterial prediction, the relevant information may concern structural patterns, organism-dependent responses or the way heterogeneous measurements should be interpreted.',
 16:'A distinct line of work treats human expertise as supervision. Expert judgements of drug-likeness helped motivate QED (27), while studies of medicinal-chemist agreement and selection bias show why the collection and aggregation of such judgements require documentation (28,29). MolSkill learns medicinal-chemist preferences for compound evaluation (30), and human feedback has also been used in molecular design and dedicated evaluation interfaces (31,32). Preference learning outside chemistry provides further examples of models trained from categorical feedback (33,34). Here we ask whether coarse Good/Bad assessments can guide a molecular representation subsequently used to predict continuous antibacterial measurements. The comparison is with quantitative guidance on the same endpoint setting, rather than only with an untrained scoring rule.',
 17:'Transfer learning and multitask approaches offer another route for sharing information between related biological endpoints, including antimicrobial-peptide classification and MIC regression (35,36). In QAC prediction, the species and endpoint type define related but distinct tasks. A shared predictor can learn their statistical relationships when the task identifier is supplied alongside the molecular representation. Text embeddings provide an alternative to categorical identifiers, as demonstrated by assay-description modelling (78). With only four endpoint descriptions, however, successful prediction does not itself show that the model understands their biological meaning; arbitrary and permuted task-code controls can test this interpretation.',
 18:'The nature of the supervision remains a central limitation. Bioactivity corpora assembled from the literature are sparse and heterogeneous across organisms, laboratories and assay protocols. Studies of mixed IC50 and Ki data demonstrate that pooling measurements from different sources can introduce substantial uncertainty (37–39). The same concern motivates careful treatment of antibacterial corpora, although the uncertainty observed for those other endpoints cannot be assigned numerically to MIC/MBC without additional evidence. An expert call may summarise several measurements or practical criteria; whether it actually harmonises assays, and whether it contributes information unavailable in the quantitative data, depends on the annotation records and information shown to the annotators.',
 20:'Our previous work established a framework for QAC MIC and MBC prediction using MolFormer-derived representations and biology-informed stacking (53). The present study asks where the useful representation guidance comes from. We first retain the original comparison between expert-guided and quantitatively guided encoders, the task-aware prediction results and the synthesis and evaluation of 17 new QACs. We then test the associated hypotheses with reconstructed v5 decoding, grouped evaluation on the original 105-molecule cohort, matched supervision and shuffled-label controls, graph and tabular alternatives, and direct versus representation-mediated adaptation. Finally, similarity, attribution and weight interventions examine which aspects of the learned representation contribute to prediction. These analyses have different evidence scopes: reported original performance, newly executed follow-ups and computational interventions are identified explicitly.',
 23:'Figure 1. Formation and use of guided molecular embeddings. A SMILES structure is encoded by frozen MolFormer into 768 features; a separate trainable encoder produces a 256-dimensional vector. Human and quantitative supervision denote alternative training objectives. The downstream decoder combines the learned vector with a species/endpoint code to predict log2 concentrations. The coloured strips display the first 24 coordinates of actual archived vectors with independently rescaled opacity; they are not a structural/biological variance partition. The ACS 1996 example is target_29136ba3d6e0 from the complete QAC register in Table S19.',
 25:'2.1 Learning the guided molecular representation',
 27:'The expert-guidance archive contains final Good/Bad calls for 1,103 compounds before the exclusions used in the new matched comparison. These calls are intended to summarise expert assessment of antibacterial suitability. The earlier draft described an approximately 1,500-compound collection and a three-expert blind majority-vote procedure; those collection-stage details require confirmation from the contributors. The final-label archive establishes the available supervisory outcomes, while the collection process, visible information and agreement are reserved for coauthor completion in Section 4.3.1. No unrecorded votes, blinding or annotation costs are assumed.',
 28:'The original descriptor screen compared MolFormer (768 dimensions), ChemBERTa, Morgan and MACCS representations using stratified classification cross-validation (Supporting Information, Figures S1–S4). The preserved SI plot reports F1=0.665 for MolFormer with an MLP and StandardScaler; we quote this as a figure-reported screening result, not an independently replayed benchmark. The original text instead gave 0.655 and reported accuracy 0.856. The numerical discrepancy remains recorded in SI pending the underlying classification runs. This screen motivated the use of MolFormer features, but aggregate F1 and accuracy alone do not establish that other representations lack antibacterial information or that the selected backbone recognises membrane-disruption mechanisms.',
 29:'The original guidance stage uses a separate MLP encoder taking the 768-dimensional MolFormer vector through hidden widths 512, 384 and 256. Linear transformations, batch normalisation, ReLU activations and dropout allow the encoder to select and reorganise the pretrained features. The archived expert model was trained with supervised contrastive guidance, a hard-negative term and Borderline SMOTE with sampling strategy 0.8. Its auxiliary projection is used to compute the training loss; the 256-dimensional encoder output, rather than the auxiliary projection or the Good/Bad classifier output, is passed to the quantitative decoder. We call this output the human-guided embedding. The source classification loss does not train a numerical MIC/MBC prediction directly.',
 30:'The archived t-SNE analysis (perplexity 30) illustrates the representation before and after expert-guided refinement (Figure 2). The original test-fold analysis reports silhouette 0.007→0.108, Davies–Bouldin index 6.54→2.58 and Calinski–Harabasz score 3.60→22.08. These are descriptive measures of class-associated geometry in that analysis, not quantitative regression performance or evidence that the encoder has acquired a molecular mechanism. The original projections and classification figures are preserved with their provenance; the new 105-molecule PCA illustration in Figure 6 shows a different cohort and colours structural classes rather than activity labels.',
 35:'We next use the learned embeddings to predict log2(MIC) and log2(MBC) for S. aureus ATCC 43300 and E. coli ATCC 25922 on the QAC dataset introduced previously (53). Table 1 retains the original v5 model comparison. The task-aware models receive both the molecular representation and the identity of the organism/endpoint pair. The recovered May notebook uses CatBoost’s native categorical task_id for the row described originally as one-hot multitask CatBoost. Literal four-coordinate one-hot codes are therefore treated separately in the new checks. The shared model uses four measurement rows per molecule, which can improve statistical sharing; this design also requires molecule-level splitting when the claim concerns new compounds.',
 36:'The original text-code variant concatenates an OpenAI task-description vector with the molecular embedding. Recovered May 24 notebook outputs give R²=0.7494, 0.6752, 0.5918 and 0.7708 for the four endpoints, supporting the positive results reported in v5. E. coli MIC is displayed here as 0.59, correcting the original rounding to 0.60. The corresponding native categorical model outputs are 0.7102, 0.6403, 0.4269 and 0.7394. These saved outputs document those original R² values; they do not by themselves reproduce every Table 1 RMSE, descriptor row or Table 2 quantitative-source model. The missing historical task-vector cache and the split structure are addressed in the follow-up checks rather than used to replace these original numbers.',
 38:'Table 1. Original v5 comparison for prediction of log2(MIC) and log2(MBC). Values are retained as reported, with the documented E. coli MIC R² rounding correction. Notebook-output provenance supports eight multitask R² values; the remaining rows and RMSE values require their corresponding run records. These heterogeneous historical rows are not presented as a single newly executed matched benchmark.',
 41:'The original multitask comparison shows why both representation guidance and task information merit investigation. Figure 3 retains the original parity panels and their printed metrics. Their relative performance motivates the subsequent checks; prediction on unseen organisms or activity types was not evaluated by the four-endpoint experiment and is not inferred from it.',
 43:'Figure 3. Original v5 parity panels for quantitative antibacterial prediction. This is a preserved historical figure. The panel labelled one-hot refers to the original categorically coded comparison; the recovered May CatBoost cell uses native categorical task_id. Printed performance labels and the historical test split belong to the original analysis, whereas the newly reconstructed and grouped results are reported separately in Section 2.5.',
 45:'A central question is whether expert Good/Bad supervision can compete with quantitative activity measurements in shaping the molecular representation. The original study trained an architecturally related encoder using multi-output regression on a larger activity collection. Table 2 retains the resulting reported comparison. This full-corpus quantitative model is distinct from the QAC-only binned contrastive ablation in Section 2.4 and from the new matched source panel. Its exact saved multitarget checkpoint has not yet been located, so that original comparison has reported-result status rather than a completed execution replay. The missing checkpoint is not evidence that the original Table 2 was generated by the QAC-binned ablation.',
 46:'The broad quantitative collection described in v5 contains 1,559 unique molecules and more than 18,000 activity records across approximately 400 strains, including MIC, MBC, MBIC and MBEC. S. aureus and E. coli comprise approximately 40% of the reported measurements. The recovered MIC-only computational export contains 9,338 records for 1,153 molecules. These counts describe different units and subsets, and are therefore reported separately. Repeated records supply assay observations rather than the same number of independent molecular structures. Assay context is retained where available; its heterogeneity motivates a harmonisation hypothesis but does not establish that the expert calls perform that harmonisation.',
 47:'In the original text-code configuration, the reported human-guided and quantitative-guided R² values are 0.75 versus 0.78 for S. aureus MIC, 0.68 versus 0.68 for S. aureus MBC, 0.59 versus 0.58 for E. coli MIC and 0.77 versus 0.77 for E. coli MBC (Table 2). Thus the original human-guided model is competitive in these reported results despite its coarser supervisory labels. The small difference for E. coli MIC is descriptive and is not a demonstrated superiority or statistical equivalence result. Comparing roughly 1,100 final calls with roughly 10,000 MIC records also does not establish a tenfold reduction in independent data or annotation cost. New matched checks ask whether the label assignment contributes beyond architecture, exposure to source molecules and the quantitative objective.',
 48:'A possible explanation is that a Good/Bad call summarises multiple aspects of a compound’s antibacterial profile. If annotators integrated activity measurements and practical criteria, the resulting call would be a compact human interpretation of those inputs. If they saw the same quantitative measurements used by the comparison model, it would be a compressed measurement-derived channel rather than independent human information. Whether either account describes the actual collection depends on H1–H7; the predictive comparison does not establish annotation information access, implicit assay harmonisation or lower acquisition cost.',
 50:'Table 2. Original v5 reported comparison of expert-guided and full-corpus quantitative-guided embeddings. The expert R² rounding is corrected as in Table 1; all other values are retained as originally reported. The full-corpus multitarget quantitative checkpoint remains unresolved. The available QAC-binned checkpoint is used only for its separately identified ablation and is not substituted for this model.',
 54:'The independent target collection comprises 17 newly synthesised QACs from four scaffold families: kojic-acid bis-QACs, tetrahydropyridine bis-QACs, CF3-dihydropyran mono-QACs and glycoluril multi-QACs (Figure 4). They cover three charge-multiplicity classes, but four chemical families. Their named identities and all measured endpoint records are retained in Supporting Information. These compounds provide a fixed OOD evaluation set for the new calculations, with no target compound used for model selection. IT505 E. coli MBC is reported as >500 mg/L and remains a right-censored endpoint, leaving 67 exact values and one bound.',
 56:'Figure 4. The four OOD chemical families represented by exact archived structures redrawn with ACS 1996 bond and atom-label settings. The complete 17-compound register and counterion treatment are given in Supporting Information.',
 78:'Scheme 4. Synthesis of glycoluril-based multi-QACs.',
 81:'The original OOD comparison examines human-guided embeddings, raw MolFormer features and descriptor-based models (Figure 5). In that archived analysis, human-guided embeddings provide the strongest numerical performance among the compared representations in the label-free setting. Here label-free denotes the absence of a species/endpoint code as an input feature, not the absence of numerical target training. Reported classification and ±1-class agreement are retained as historical analyses. They address different losses from quantitative RMSE and are not interchangeable with regression accuracy.',
 82:'The original zero-shot/few-shot comparison also evaluates adaptation with approximately 40% of the OOD data. In this terminology, zero-shot means that a QAC-trained predictor is evaluated without fitting on the new 17 compounds; it does not mean direct MIC prediction from a source Good/Bad classifier. The few-shot analysis is an exploratory target-adaptation experiment and no longer an untouched OOD test. Its original figure and learning curves are preserved. All new OOD results in Sections 2.5–2.8 instead fit on the 105-QAC cohort and keep all 17 new compounds out of training and selection.',
 84:'Figure 5. Preserved original v5 OOD and few-shot comparisons. Zero-shot denotes no OOD-target adaptation of the QAC-trained predictor; few-shot uses part of the OOD collection and is exploratory. Historical treatment of the censored measurement is not assumed to match the exact/bound treatment in the new endpoint registry.',
 86:'How much of the guided representation reflects pretrained geometry, and which of its changes matter for prediction? These questions require separate measurements. Figure 6 combines the representation pathway with actual PCA projections for the 105 QACs. It illustrates the reorganisation of the coordinates; it does not divide the embedding into experimentally identified structural and biological components.',
 88:'Figure 6. Actual archived raw and human-guided representations of all 105 QACs. The two panels use independent PCA after per-molecule L2 normalisation; their axes are not aligned. Colours and markers denote mono-, bis- and multi-QAC structural classes, not Good/Bad activity. The projections are unsupervised visualisations of archived representations whose source corpora overlap this cohort; they are not held-out predictive or causal evidence.',
 89:'The archived representation analysis reports linear CKA of 0.933 in-domain and 0.946 on OOD compounds. A linear probe predicts the human-guided coordinates from MolFormer features with a mean explained variance of 0.891. These values show substantial geometric alignment and linear recoverability. Because the guided encoder is itself a function of the MolFormer features, high recoverability is compatible with useful nonlinear reorganisation. The unexplained portion cannot be labelled biological knowledge, and CKA does not establish how much unique activity information either representation contains.',
 90:'The original combined-feature SHAP analysis reports block attribution totals of 2.93 [2.89,2.97] for MolFormer, 0.76 [0.63,0.89] for the task identifier and 0.64 [0.62,0.65] for the guided embedding, with an embedding-to-MolFormer ratio of 0.22 [0.21,0.22]. These are retained as archive-specific descriptive results. Correlated feature blocks can redistribute attribution, so the ratio is not a causal information fraction and division by the number of coordinates does not make the blocks independent. Predictive ablations and controls are needed to determine whether the representation or its supervisory labels contribute to held-out loss.',
 91:'The original QAC-only ablation uses quantitative MIC/MBC binned into contrastive classes and is distinct from the full-corpus multi-output model in Table 2. The July notebook reports OOD RMSE of 2.314 for raw features, 2.189 for human-guided embeddings and 2.133 for this QAC-binned ablation. A different May version gives 2.276 for the latter; both are retained in the provenance record. Table 3 instead evaluates vectors reconstructed from the available analysis/encoder_reg_only.pth checkpoint, under the declared new 105-QAC training and 67-exact-endpoint protocol, giving RMSE 2.408 with fresh text codes. Neither that checkpoint’s identity with the July model nor the exact origin of the archive-version gap is established, so these are distinct calculations rather than replacement values. The original post-hoc CausalForestDML analysis reports a residual-based average contrast of −0.015 log2 units with interval [−0.43,+0.32]. It describes the chosen model comparisons under its assumptions, rather than identifying a biological intervention. It is also different from the archived endpoint-resampling bootstrap ΔRMSE analysis. An interval covering zero and the use of assay dilution units do not by themselves prove formal equivalence or absence of a meaningful subgroup effect.',
 92:'The original conditional-effect summary reports no clearly resolved subgroup advantage on the small OOD set. That observation motivates further work on heterogeneous effects, but 17 compounds from four families provide limited independent support for subgroup claims. Whether measured protocol heterogeneity explains any difference between the supervisory sources remains a hypothesis. Testing it would require context-matched comparisons, annotation information-access records and appropriate resampling of independent molecules or assay contexts.',
 93:'The original findings therefore motivate a focused predictive interpretation: a compact encoder can reshape pretrained molecular features into a representation useful to a downstream antibacterial predictor, and coarse expert guidance can be competitive with quantitative guidance in the reported configuration. Stronger attribution to the semantic content of human judgement requires controls beyond geometry and attribution. We next examine these controls while keeping the v5 architecture and original positive results explicit.',
 95:'This study combines expert-guided molecular representation learning with quantitative prediction and the synthesis and evaluation of 17 new QACs. The original v5 multitask results remain a substantive part of the evidence: the human-guided text-code model reports R² of 0.75,0.68,0.59 and 0.77 and is competitive with the reported full-corpus quantitative-guided model. These values have partial saved-notebook provenance and are retained alongside, rather than replaced by, new validation results.',
 96:'First, the compact external encoder gives a concrete route for adapting frozen molecular features. Its predictive usefulness depends on the source supervision, task encoding and downstream decoder. The new OOD comparison of archived human-guided and raw text-code features gives RMSE 2.023 versus 2.245. That fixed-checkpoint result supports predictive utility in this setting, while grouped evaluation demonstrates that the original task-row holdout answers a different generalisation question from prediction on new cations and scaffolds.',
 97:'Second, the controlled compact-encoder experiment does not establish that human label content is responsible for its predictive benefit. Human guidance improves OOD loss relative to the fixed raw reference, but does not outperform shuffled human labels, and parameter-reset intervals cover zero. Separate partial-backbone experiments resolve a benefit under a different adaptation procedure. These findings support a conditional research programme for human guidance, not universal superiority, a guaranteed measurement-replacement rate or a proof of biological causality.',
 98:'Third, modern decoders and direct fine-tuning provide necessary context for the practical choice of model. None of the tested tabular models consistently improves both grouped-validation and OOD loss on the saved v5 representation. Full fine-tuning is competitive on cation holdout but performs poorly on OOD compounds in the declared direct-prediction budget. The central conclusion is therefore about the usefulness and limits of representation guidance in a small antibacterial dataset. Establishing an independent human-information channel, confirming the full-corpus quantitative model and assessing unseen assay contexts remain essential next steps.',
 187:'The full 105-QAC and 17-OOD registers, exact/bound endpoint registry, notebook-output provenance, input hashes, split definitions, training settings and saved per-molecule predictions are assembled with the revision. Public immutable archive identifiers and redistribution conditions will be completed in the coauthor fields below.',
 193:'Supporting Information contains the four original author figures, the extended experimental and computational records, the full target compound registers and endpoint predictions, and all original and subsequent hypothesis checks. Archived analyses retain their original reference numbering within explicitly labelled sections.'
 }
 for i,text in replacements.items():h.replace(old[i],text,marked=False)
 # Fix only two explicit source-table items, without changing unrecovered metrics.
 edits=[]
 for table in [old[39],old[51]]:
  for cell in table.findall('.//w:tc',NS):
   text=h.text(cell);new=text.replace('ATCC 4300','ATCC 43300').replace('0.95 (R2=0.60)','0.95 (R2=0.59)')
