 new=[p('2.5 Reconstructing v5 and defining the generalisation question',2),
 p('The May notebook saves the strong v5 multitask R² values, while its split is applied to molecule×task rows with 20% held out, random_state=42 and stratification by task. Other measurements of a test-row molecule can therefore remain in decoder training. This evaluates a different problem from predicting a wholly unseen molecule. It does not invalidate the existence of the saved outputs, and the difference from later grouped scores is not attributed solely to this split: feature extraction, checkpoint state, source exclusions, text vectors and software versions also changed.'),
 p('We reconstructed nine original-108 configurations from the saved raw, human and QAC-binned features with CatBoost 500 iterations, depth 6 and learning rate 0.05. The two supplied servers independently executed these jobs and reproduced each other’s predictions to within 1e−10. This establishes reproducibility of the declared new reconstruction, not an exact replay of every historical cell. The historical OpenAI vectors were not located; the reconstruction uses the separately documented cached October task vectors. For example, the reconstructed human/text endpoint R² values are 0.728,0.380,0.775 and 0.708, rather than the saved May values. Both sets remain identified in Supporting Information.'),
 p('For new-compound evaluation, we retain all 105 QACs and use fixed five-fold cation and scaffold holdouts, yielding 420 exact endpoint predictions in each regime. The cohort includes 103 distinct cations and 29 cation scaffolds. No target cation or scaffold crosses its corresponding training/test boundary. The archived expert source corpus contains structures from all 105 QAC salts; corpus membership is not proof of training membership for every old checkpoint, and this archived-feature test is not claimed to be source-excluded transfer. The new matched panel in Section 2.6 excludes target cations and their related records before source training.'),
 p('Table 3 shows the follow-up on archived v5 representations. Human/text features give OOD RMSE 2.023 versus 2.245 for raw/text features, whereas their cation/scaffold losses are similar. Literal one-hot, native categorical and text codes have different results; a text-code advantage on OOD is not a universal advantage across splits. The complete endpoint-specific values, including negative R² where present, are retained in SI.'),
 p('Table 3. Newly executed grouped and OOD validation of archived v5 representations. Pooled exact-endpoint RMSE in log2(mg/L); source overlap is disclosed. Human features use encoder_borderline_smote.pth (SHA256 05a54c4f1d49d7b4…); QAC-binned features use analysis/encoder_reg_only.pth (358ccc8f54bd2bef…). The latter is not the full-corpus model from Table 2, and is not established as the exact July checkpoint.'),h.table(template,legacy),
 p('2.6 Matched guidance in the compact v5 architecture',2),
 p('To test label assignment while retaining the v5 encoder widths, we trained six source arms on the same 681 source molecules: 377 training and 304 validation compounds, with QAC/OOD target cations excluded. Human calls, prevalence-matched binary MIC and continuous pooled MIC each have a source-training-only shuffled control. Five paired seeds share initial states, minibatch order and 80 epochs (480 updates). Human and binary MIC share the hard-negative contrastive objective; continuous MIC uses standardised MSE and therefore changes both supervision and objective. No SMOTE, augmentation, early stopping or target-based selection is used in this controlled follow-up; it is not a replay of historical SMOTE training.'),
 p('The encoder has 691,584 trainable parameters. Including the active contrastive projection gives 741,504 parameters for the binary arms; the encoder plus scalar regression head gives 691,841 for the continuous arms. The source-only auxiliary heads are removed from target decoding. The fixed CatBoost decoder uses the recovered v5 budget across arms, and no OOD outcomes are used for tuning. Table 4 reports the mean of per-seed RMSE rather than the RMSE of an ensemble prediction.'),
 p('Human, binary-MIC and continuous-MIC cation RMSE values are 1.872,1.865 and 1.858; their OOD values are 2.268,2.276 and 2.474. Human minus continuous MIC on OOD is '+ci('human minus mic_continuous','full-QAC105')+' under paired source-seed/scaffold resampling, which leaves the direction unresolved at this interval level. Human minus the fixed raw reference is '+ci('human minus matched_raw','full-QAC105')+'. These contrasts address prediction under this protocol, not formal equivalence of supervisory sources.'),
 p('The primary content control is human versus shuffled human labels. Its cation/scaffold/OOD ΔRMSE values are '+', '.join(ci('human minus human_shuffled',s) for s in ['cation','scaffold','full-QAC105'])+'. Human guidance does not beat its shuffled control here. Compression, exposure to the same source feature distribution and BatchNorm running statistics may contribute to the benefit relative to raw features. Noise-induced regularisation and the fit of the chosen encoder/objective are additional candidate explanations, not identified mechanisms. The earlier source-excluded partial-MolFormer experiment resolves a cation benefit for real human labels (ΔRMSE −0.120 [−0.217,−0.043]), but it changes the adaptation procedure and is retained as a separate supporting experiment rather than proof about this compact encoder.'),
 p('A supplementary task-code check fixes human compact seed 0 and substitutes only the four task vectors. OOD RMSE is 2.314 with the cached text codes, 2.292 with a fixed permutation of those codes and 2.327 with norm-matched random codes; literal one-hot coding gives 2.255. The complete three-regime comparison is in Table S39. With four repeatedly observed tasks and one seed, these point estimates do not establish a semantic advantage of the task descriptions. Generalisation to unseen task descriptions was not tested.'),
 p('Table 4. Compact v5-architecture source controls, with native categorical target coding. Mean per-seed pooled exact-endpoint RMSE; five paired source seeds. Raw features form a fixed reference. Complete shuffled quantitative controls are included.'),h.table(template,controls),
 v.embed(files,ROOT/'figures/figure7_compact_real_shuffled_violins.png','rIdV18check7'),
 p('Figure 7. Absolute-error distributions for the compact encoder with real and shuffled source labels, using a shared y-axis. Each value is a compound/endpoint mean absolute error over five source seeds; distributions contain 420 QAC or 67 uncensored OOD endpoints. Dots denote medians; all tails are retained. The violins are descriptive, while clustered performance contrasts are given in the text and SI.'),
 p('2.7 Decoder and direct-adaptation alternatives',2),
 p('We tested TabPFN v2, Google TabFM and AutoGluon on the saved v5 raw and human-guided representations (80–82). Each endpoint is fitted separately on the same target folds and untouched OOD collection. The modern models use training-only PCA with 32 components and their declared model-specific budgets; the CatBoost reference uses the full vectors. These are practical pipeline comparisons rather than identical-capacity or identical-compute experiments. The completed 264 fits comprise three decoders × two representations × four endpoints × eleven regimes (five cation folds, five scaffold folds and one full-QAC/OOD fit). Table 5 includes all raw/guided outcomes, rather than selecting the preferred representation from OOD performance.'),
 p('None of the modern tabular pipelines consistently improves the saved v5 CatBoost representation pipeline across the three regimes (Figure 8). For example, TabPFN with raw features gives OOD RMSE 2.058, while its human-guided counterpart gives 2.165. AutoGluon gives 2.163 versus 2.081, so a human-guided improvement depends on the decoder. Chemprop’s graph-prediction benchmark (84) and RDKit alternatives on the same 105/17 target registers are retained in SI; those comparisons use the documented structural inputs and earlier adaptation setting and are not silently relabelled as v5-encoder experiments.'),
 p('Table 5. Separate-endpoint decoders applied to archived v5 representations. Pooled exact-endpoint RMSE; one source checkpoint. Modern pipelines include train-only PCA32, whereas CatBoost uses the full representation.'),h.table(template,modernrows),
 v.embed(files,ROOT/'figures/figure8_v5_modern_decoders.png','rIdV18check8'),
 p('Figure 8. Modern decoder comparisons on the saved v5 features, with separate endpoint models for every method. Points show pooled RMSE on exact endpoints only (67 uncensored OOD values); raw and human-guided representations are shown for all methods. Each pipeline uses one archived source checkpoint; source-seed variability is not estimated and no uncertainty bars are shown. The x-axis starts above zero to show the observed range. Differences between preprocessing and model budgets are described in the text.'),
 p('To assess fine-tuning rather than assume it is undesirable, the separate direct-prediction experiment compares a pretrained frozen linear head, LoRA, full backbone fine-tuning and source-human-guided partial fine-tuning (79). The same three source/target seeds are used for the two human-guided prediction routes in Table 6. Full direct fine-tuning gives cation RMSE 1.863 but OOD RMSE 3.032. The partial-human direct predictor gives OOD 2.412, versus 2.106 when that guided representation is frozen and decoded with CatBoost. This supports the two-stage route under the declared budget. It does not show that full fine-tuning is generally poor, nor is the partial backbone the external compact v5 encoder.'),
 p('Table 6. Previously completed direct backbone-adaptation check, retained as a separate hypothesis test. Pooled exact-endpoint RMSE; the human direct and frozen-decoder rows use the same three source seeds. Source adaptation, backbone extraction and target budgets differ from Tables 3–5.'),h.table(template,routes),
 p('2.8 Learned updates, label content and the scope of causality',2),
 p('For the compact encoder, a parameter-only reset restores the initial learned parameters while keeping the trained BatchNorm running means and variances. A complete initial-state reference also restores those buffers. With the downstream decoder refitted under the same folds, parameter-only reset minus intact human cation/scaffold/OOD ΔRMSE is '+', '.join(ci('human_weight_only minus human',s) for s in ['cation','scaffold','full-QAC105'])+'. All these scaffold-clustered intervals include zero. Thus this experiment does not resolve a necessary contribution of the compact encoder’s learned parameter updates. The retained BatchNorm state can still carry source-training information, so parameter-only reset is not described as removal of all learned knowledge.'),
 p('The distinct partial-backbone rewind experiment reports a cation loss increase of +0.109 [+0.037,+0.201] on restoring learned weights. Its OOD interval is +0.243 [−0.067,+0.582]. These results concern an intervention on a computational prediction pipeline: they can test whether a particular learned update contributes to held-out loss under fixed controls. They do not identify antibacterial mechanisms or demonstrate that a named chemical concept was learned (83). Real-versus-shuffled source labels test supervisory assignment; weight resets test update dependence; source exclusions test transfer. Evidence for independent human knowledge additionally depends on annotation information access in H1–H7.'),
 p('A causal claim is consequently restricted to the declared computational intervention and the contrasts its uncertainty resolves. The full-corpus quantitative model, original assay-context heterogeneity and 17-compound subgroup analysis remain distinct evidence streams. Neither pointwise intervals covering zero nor comparison with a twofold dilution justify a formal equivalence conclusion without a justified equivalence margin and appropriate design. All hypotheses and unresolved provenance items are mapped to their analyses in Supporting Information.')]
